  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nA cold front has brought temperatures down a bit today, and some\nlow to mid level clouds have helped keep it cooler as well. The\ntrend toward cooler temperatures is expected through Tuesday as\nthe main Pacific trough makes its way in. The rainfall totals have\ndecreased a tad for Sunday through Tuesday, but the overall\npattern continues to show rainfall amounts over half an inch in\nthe north up to 1.6 inches; this area includes Phillips, Valley,\nDaniels, Sheridan, and Roosevelt counties. Garfield, Petroleum,\nMcCone, Dawson, Richland, Wibaux, and Prairie counties are\nsuggested to see a few hundredths up to 1 inch. The southeast\ncounties have the lowest forecast rain amounts. Most of the rain\nis expected to occur on Sunday night.\n\nAn airmass on the humid side is expected to move in from the east\ntomorrow to bring showers and isolated strong thunderstorms to the\narea. SPC has a marginal risk of severe weather that may be\nupgraded to a slight risk at some point.\n\nAlthough there is some uncertainty on how much wind there will be\nas the system departs to the east, strong winds with gusts over 45\nmph appears likely for the entire area on Tuesday. This is \nbecause as the Fall season makes its way in, the polar jet stream \ntypically starts strengthening again. Increases in differential \nheating (difference between cold and warm air) occur as \ntemperatures in the north decrease, especially with shorter days \nand lower sun angles. If you are interested, a few terms used in\nmeteorology are geostrophic wind, ageostrophic wind, and thermal\nwind. Each one has a specific meaning and are helpful in\nunderstanding weather systems. Anyway, a lake wind advisory will\nmost definitely be needed for Monday night into Tuesday.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nMade slight increase in PoPs through tomorrow morning.\n\nThere is moderate to high (50 to 80 percent) confidence in rain\namounts from Sunday through Tuesday, and winds for Tuesday. \n\nThere is low confidence in the location of severe thunderstorm \nactivity tomorrow evening into the overnight.",
